  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-98161",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Primary Roots of Unity",
      "problem": "Find the principal $7$th root of unity.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{7}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/7}",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
