  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-216710",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/b575a8b3c4830aa48586b457b45f76ca8df49ecf8036df720f66aaddc857bdaf.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above.",
      "worked_solution": "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $6$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $6$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/34a3bb77a9765cd806a047ad42a7f0719201effdd46659efe7ffd1a94a051910.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{(5)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
