  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-216706",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/89d0606f11701cb59685e5ea1540b8ed98ce8cfe371951a675f01a1c6cc5a748.png)\n\nFind the $7$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above.",
      "worked_solution": "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/344d760f2cd08e5e1e1eccc2664e7182d01744ee9b0b15f985c6d7b4e0e5515d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
