      "problem": "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the value of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $4$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})=6.$",
